*(
    title: Complex Numbers
    description: Complex numbers are numbers that have both a real part and an imaginary part. They can be represented in the form a + bi, where a is the real part, b is the imaginary part, and i is the imaginary unit (the square root of -1).
    tags: complex numbers, complex analysis
    lesson
)

scene{
    Imagine a number line that extends infinitely in both directions. On this number line, we can represent real numbers, such as 1, -2, and 3. But what if we want to represent numbers that are not on this line? This is where complex numbers come in.

    definiton{
        A **complex number** is a number of the form $a + bi$, where:
        - $a$ is the real part
        - $b$ is the imaginary part
        - $i$ is the imaginary unit, defined as $i^2 = -1$
    }(
        term: Complex Number
    )

    Complex numbers allow us to extend the concept of numbers beyond the real number line. They can be visualized in a two-dimensional plane, known as the complex plane, where the horizontal axis represents the real part and the vertical axis represents the imaginary part.
}